$DIR/test.p:34:31: invalid operation: cannot take address of v[0]: it is a copy returned by the Index method of *Vec
    x[k] on a container calls its Index method, which returns a copy of the
    element. A copy has no address that would stay valid, and a pointer to the
    element itself would be invalidated as soon as the container grows or
    rehashes and moves its elements.
    Call a method that returns the pointer explicitly, such as vec.Vec.Ptr or
    hash.Map.Ptr. Its documentation says how long the pointer is valid.
    &s[i] on a slice is safe because a slice never moves its elements. A
    container that grows does, so Plutonium makes the pointer an explicit method
    call rather than give it the same syntax as the safe case.
    Ask for the pointer by name:
        p := v.Ptr(i)
        p.count++
    (see "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.11)
    (run "pluto explain E2454" for a longer explanation)
